{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1794248093,"nonce":"UErImXx/ibzJbM7ZTaDO1NnejLg=","target":"33fc3ab5a35dc14fafe7bee6de0403e9d88b508c6b072c3d74e6e658cd40b162","message":"Rewritten 2026-11-09 (owner): the canary repo lives on next.forgejo.org and drives the public demo. It replaces the 2026-11-01 proposal (a repo on the in-cluster Forgejo of the local dogfood stack). The audit below (comment #1) was of that proposal; its points on the webhook secret and the public URL no longer apply, and its dispatch-only alternative is dropped because the point is the real webhook path from a real forge.\n\n\nThe demo only gets traffic when someone pushes to its GitHub repo, so it drifts unnoticed. 07b6d12 shows the cost: deliveries failed from 2026-10-28 to 2026-11-09 and nothing said so. A repo on a public Forgejo that commits on a schedule gives the demo traffic around the clock, through Forgejo's real webhook delivery, a real https clone with forge_token, and a Forgejo version that changes daily.\n\n\n- Forge: https://next.forgejo.org, Forgejo's official test instance. It updates to the newest Forgejo daily and its data may be wiped without notice (https://forgejo.org/docs/next/user/forgejo-next/). Codeberg was ruled out: its terms are FOSS-only, ban projects mostly written by AI, and refuse projects that use far more CI than comparable ones written by people.\n- Scarab: the demo (https://demo-scarab.ahiravan.dev). It is the only Scarab that is always up and reachable from the internet.\n\n\n1. Owner, by hand: an account on next.forgejo.org, an owner/canary repo, and a token with repository write scope. The token goes on the demo box as a Kubernetes Secret, never in the repo.\n2. Demo: set SCARAB_FORGEJO_WEBHOOK_SECRET (the server log says \"forgejo webhook: disabled\" today), add a Forgejo connection for https://next.forgejo.org, and bind the canary repo in its own Project so canary Runs can be filtered out.\n3. A script under deploy/demo-oracle/ that creates or repairs the repo contents, the webhook and the bind, and is safe to run again. It must run again after a wipe of next.forgejo.org, so it cannot assume anything exists.\n4. A CronJob in the scarab namespace on the demo box: every 30 min it commits one file through the Forgejo contents API (PUT /api/v1/repos/{owner}/{repo}/contents/{path}). Image pinned by digest. When the repo is gone (a wipe) it fails, and the failure says to run the script in step 3.\n5. The canary pipeline, steps busybox-sized: checkout through lib:thulasi-ram/clone with forge_token, a library invoke, results and artifacts, a matrix, and one step that fails on a seeded fraction of commits so retries happen. No manual gate: every Run must end.\n6. A check that a Run followed each commit: the next CronJob tick asks the Scarab API whether the previous commit's Run reached a terminal state, and fails otherwise. Where that failure is surfaced is open question 1.\n\n\n- Fix 986542a first: finished step Pods are never deleted, and the canary adds about 48 Runs a day to a 2-core box that already holds 472 leftover Pods.\n- The demo org must have lib:thulasi-ram/clone installed (it did not on 2026-11-09; see 07b6d12).\n\n\n1. Where does a failed check go so someone sees it? Options: a GitHub issue opened by a workflow, a Pushover/ntfy notification, or a status badge on the demo page.\n2. Retention: Run metadata is never pruned (comment #1), so 48 Runs a day grows the demo database without bound. Shorter TTLs for the canary Project, or a periodic delete of old canary Runs?\n3. Some failures will be bugs in Forgejo's daily build, not in Scarab. Is that acceptable noise, or should the canary also run against a pinned Forgejo release?\n\n\n- A canary Run appears on the demo at least every 30 min without anyone pushing, for 24 h.\n- Every canary Run reaches a terminal state.\n- The check in step 6 fails within one tick when deliveries stop (test it by disabling the webhook on next.forgejo.org).\n- After a wipe of next.forgejo.org, running the step 3 script restores the canary with no other manual step.\n- No new top-level just recipe (c043abf).","files":null}]}